1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,985
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,165 --> 00:00:04,584
Every person watching this video is on

3
00:00:04,584 --> 00:00:06,240
one of seven money levels.

4
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:08,220
Not because of how much they earn,

5
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:10,020
but because of what their money is

6
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:10,867
doing right now.

7
00:00:11,047 --> 00:00:13,310
Some people are fighting debt every month.

8
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:15,720
Some people never have to work again.

9
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:18,090
And most people are stuck somewhere in

10
00:00:18,090 --> 00:00:20,742
the middle without knowing which level they're

11
00:00:20,742 --> 00:00:22,356
on, or what it takes to move

12
00:00:22,356 --> 00:00:22,529
up.

13
00:00:22,879 --> 00:00:23,923
Seven levels.

14
00:00:24,103 --> 00:00:25,834
From owing more than you own,

15
00:00:25,834 --> 00:00:27,624
all the way to money that pays

16
00:00:27,624 --> 00:00:28,817
for your whole life.

17
00:00:28,997 --> 00:00:30,854
By the end of this video you'll

18
00:00:30,854 --> 00:00:32,472
know exactly where you are,

19
00:00:32,472 --> 00:00:34,269
and the one move that gets you

20
00:00:34,269 --> 00:00:35,348
to the next level.

21
00:00:35,698 --> 00:00:37,497
Here's the surprising part.

22
00:00:37,677 --> 00:00:39,603
Your level has very little to do

23
00:00:39,603 --> 00:00:40,626
with your salary.

24
00:00:40,806 --> 00:00:42,931
There are doctors on level one,

25
00:00:42,931 --> 00:00:43,960
buried in debt,

26
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:46,634
and there are mechanics and teachers on

27
00:00:46,634 --> 00:00:47,319
level six.

28
00:00:47,499 --> 00:00:49,426
Income helps, of course.

29
00:00:49,606 --> 00:00:51,412
But the level you're on is decided

30
00:00:51,412 --> 00:00:53,271
by what happens to your money after

31
00:00:53,271 --> 00:00:53,856
it arrives.

32
00:00:54,205 --> 00:00:56,249
And each level has its own trap.

33
00:00:56,429 --> 00:00:59,086
A mistake that feels completely normal at

34
00:00:59,086 --> 00:01:01,679
that level, but keeps people stuck there

35
00:01:01,679 --> 00:01:02,327
for years.

36
00:01:02,507 --> 00:01:04,419
We'll look at all seven traps,

37
00:01:04,419 --> 00:01:07,095
with real numbers from the Federal Reserve

38
00:01:07,095 --> 00:01:08,625
and other named sources,

39
00:01:08,625 --> 00:01:10,728
so you can skip the mistakes most

40
00:01:10,728 --> 00:01:11,493
people make.

41
00:01:11,842 --> 00:01:13,456
So let's find out where you are.

42
00:01:13,636 --> 00:01:15,008
Grab a piece of paper,

43
00:01:15,008 --> 00:01:17,003
or just keep count in your head.

44
00:01:17,183 --> 00:01:18,576
Let's start at the bottom.

45
00:01:18,926 --> 00:01:20,366
Quick note on the data.

46
00:01:20,546 --> 00:01:23,120
Net worth numbers come from the Federal

47
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:25,627
Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances,

48
00:01:25,627 --> 00:01:28,596
the most detailed study of American household

49
00:01:28,596 --> 00:01:30,972
wealth, published every three years.

50
00:01:31,152 --> 00:01:33,871
The latest full survey covers twenty twenty

51
00:01:33,871 --> 00:01:34,124
two.

52
00:01:34,304 --> 00:01:36,783
We also use the Federal Reserve's survey

53
00:01:36,783 --> 00:01:38,270
on household well being,

54
00:01:38,270 --> 00:01:40,006
Bankrate's savings research,

55
00:01:40,006 --> 00:01:41,989
and the New York Fed's report on

56
00:01:41,989 --> 00:01:42,919
household debt.

57
00:01:43,268 --> 00:01:45,205
When we show growth over time,

58
00:01:45,205 --> 00:01:47,593
we use a seven percent average yearly

59
00:01:47,593 --> 00:01:49,271
return as an illustration.

60
00:01:49,451 --> 00:01:50,763
That is not a promise.

61
00:01:50,943 --> 00:01:53,174
Real markets go up and down,

62
00:01:53,174 --> 00:01:54,449
sometimes a lot.

63
00:01:54,629 --> 00:01:56,487
This video is education,

64
00:01:56,487 --> 00:01:58,112
not financial advice.

65
00:01:58,292 --> 00:01:59,975
Use it to understand the map,

66
00:01:59,975 --> 00:02:02,123
then make decisions that fit your own

67
00:02:02,123 --> 00:02:02,414
life.

68
00:02:02,763 --> 00:02:04,482
First, the big picture.

69
00:02:04,662 --> 00:02:06,665
According to the Federal Reserve,

70
00:02:06,665 --> 00:02:08,851
the median American family had a net

71
00:02:08,851 --> 00:02:11,097
worth of about one hundred ninety two

72
00:02:11,097 --> 00:02:13,404
thousand dollars in twenty twenty two.

73
00:02:13,584 --> 00:02:15,381
Median means half had more,

74
00:02:15,381 --> 00:02:16,312
half had less.

75
00:02:16,492 --> 00:02:18,506
But the average was over one million

76
00:02:18,506 --> 00:02:20,463
dollars, pulled up by the very top.

77
00:02:20,812 --> 00:02:22,856
That gap tells you something important.

78
00:02:23,036 --> 00:02:24,475
The average is misleading.

79
00:02:24,655 --> 00:02:26,965
Most families are much closer to the

80
00:02:26,965 --> 00:02:28,954
median, and a big share of that

81
00:02:28,954 --> 00:02:30,365
median is home equity,

82
00:02:30,365 --> 00:02:32,225
money you can't easily spend.

83
00:02:32,405 --> 00:02:34,347
So if your number feels small,

84
00:02:34,347 --> 00:02:36,742
you are probably more normal than you

85
00:02:36,742 --> 00:02:37,130
think.

86
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:39,059
Age matters a lot too.

87
00:02:39,239 --> 00:02:41,310
The same survey found the median net

88
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:43,611
worth for families under thirty five was

89
00:02:43,611 --> 00:02:45,625
about thirty nine thousand dollars.

90
00:02:45,805 --> 00:02:47,531
For thirty five to forty four,

91
00:02:47,531 --> 00:02:49,776
about one hundred thirty five thousand.

92
00:02:49,956 --> 00:02:51,778
For forty five to fifty four,

93
00:02:51,778 --> 00:02:54,228
about two hundred forty seven thousand.

94
00:02:54,577 --> 00:02:56,020
Keep those numbers in mind,

95
00:02:56,020 --> 00:02:58,051
because they'll help you see where you

96
00:02:58,051 --> 00:02:58,745
really stand.

97
00:02:58,925 --> 00:03:00,412
Now, level one.

98
00:03:00,761 --> 00:03:01,458
Level one.

99
00:03:01,638 --> 00:03:02,404
Underwater.

100
00:03:02,584 --> 00:03:03,908
You owe more than you own.

101
00:03:04,088 --> 00:03:06,259
Your net worth is below zero.

102
00:03:06,439 --> 00:03:08,335
If you sold everything you have today,

103
00:03:08,335 --> 00:03:10,082
you still couldn't pay off what you

104
00:03:10,082 --> 00:03:10,282
owe.

105
00:03:10,631 --> 00:03:12,953
Level one usually looks like this.

106
00:03:13,133 --> 00:03:15,596
Credit card balances that never really go

107
00:03:15,596 --> 00:03:15,897
down.

108
00:03:16,077 --> 00:03:18,326
A car loan bigger than what the

109
00:03:18,326 --> 00:03:19,269
car is worth.

110
00:03:19,449 --> 00:03:20,680
Student loans.

111
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:22,940
Maybe a buy now pay later plan

112
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:23,426
or two.

113
00:03:23,606 --> 00:03:25,595
Every month, a big part of the

114
00:03:25,595 --> 00:03:28,247
paycheck is already spoken for before it

115
00:03:28,247 --> 00:03:29,109
even arrives.

116
00:03:29,458 --> 00:03:31,537
And it's more common than people admit.

117
00:03:31,717 --> 00:03:34,131
The New York Fed reports that Americans

118
00:03:34,131 --> 00:03:36,730
owe more than eighteen trillion dollars in

119
00:03:36,730 --> 00:03:39,701
household debt, including well over one trillion

120
00:03:39,701 --> 00:03:41,063
on credit cards alone.

121
00:03:41,243 --> 00:03:43,658
Credit card interest rates have been above

122
00:03:43,658 --> 00:03:46,015
twenty percent, which means a balance can

123
00:03:46,015 --> 00:03:48,085
grow faster than most people can pay

124
00:03:48,085 --> 00:03:48,545
it down.

125
00:03:48,895 --> 00:03:50,691
The trap at level one is paying

126
00:03:50,691 --> 00:03:53,123
the minimum and feeling like you're making

127
00:03:53,123 --> 00:03:53,644
progress.

128
00:03:53,824 --> 00:03:56,248
On a card charging over twenty percent,

129
00:03:56,248 --> 00:03:58,921
the minimum payment mostly covers interest.

130
00:03:59,101 --> 00:04:00,975
You can pay for years and still

131
00:04:00,975 --> 00:04:02,607
owe almost the same amount.

132
00:04:02,787 --> 00:04:04,180
It feels responsible.

133
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:05,939
It's actually treading water.

134
00:04:06,289 --> 00:04:08,591
The move to level two is simple,

135
00:04:08,591 --> 00:04:09,238
not easy.

136
00:04:09,418 --> 00:04:11,415
List every debt with its interest rate.

137
00:04:11,595 --> 00:04:12,954
Stop adding new debt.

138
00:04:13,134 --> 00:04:15,314
Then throw every extra dollar at the

139
00:04:15,314 --> 00:04:16,465
highest rate first,

140
00:04:16,465 --> 00:04:18,706
while paying the minimum on the rest.

141
00:04:18,886 --> 00:04:19,925
When one is gone,

142
00:04:19,925 --> 00:04:22,126
roll that payment into the next one.

143
00:04:22,306 --> 00:04:23,745
That's the fastest way up.

144
00:04:24,095 --> 00:04:24,885
Level two.

145
00:04:25,065 --> 00:04:26,005
Paycheck to paycheck.

146
00:04:26,185 --> 00:04:27,551
You're not sinking anymore,

147
00:04:27,551 --> 00:04:29,018
but you're not moving either.

148
00:04:29,198 --> 00:04:31,077
Your net worth is around zero,

149
00:04:31,077 --> 00:04:33,456
and your bank account hits nearly zero

150
00:04:33,456 --> 00:04:35,084
right before every payday.

151
00:04:35,434 --> 00:04:37,694
Level two is where a huge number

152
00:04:37,694 --> 00:04:38,965
of Americans live,

153
00:04:38,965 --> 00:04:41,437
including people with good incomes.

154
00:04:41,617 --> 00:04:42,884
The money comes in,

155
00:04:42,884 --> 00:04:44,752
and somehow it all goes out.

156
00:04:44,932 --> 00:04:46,892
Rent, car, groceries,

157
00:04:46,892 --> 00:04:49,506
subscriptions, a dinner out.

158
00:04:49,686 --> 00:04:50,812
Nothing crazy.

159
00:04:50,992 --> 00:04:52,428
But nothing left over,

160
00:04:52,428 --> 00:04:52,884
either.

161
00:04:53,234 --> 00:04:55,766
The Federal Reserve found that sixty three

162
00:04:55,766 --> 00:04:58,177
percent of adults would cover a surprise

163
00:04:58,177 --> 00:05:00,467
four hundred dollar expense with cash.

164
00:05:00,647 --> 00:05:02,413
That means more than a third would

165
00:05:02,413 --> 00:05:02,621
not.

166
00:05:02,801 --> 00:05:05,059
And Bankrate found that about one in

167
00:05:05,059 --> 00:05:07,694
four Americans has no emergency savings at

168
00:05:07,694 --> 00:05:07,944
all.

169
00:05:08,124 --> 00:05:09,032
At level two,

170
00:05:09,032 --> 00:05:11,267
one flat tire can push you right

171
00:05:11,267 --> 00:05:12,525
back to level one.

172
00:05:12,874 --> 00:05:15,263
The trap at level two is lifestyle

173
00:05:15,263 --> 00:05:15,684
creep.

174
00:05:15,864 --> 00:05:17,431
Every raise disappears.

175
00:05:17,611 --> 00:05:19,113
A bigger apartment,

176
00:05:19,113 --> 00:05:20,062
a nicer car,

177
00:05:20,062 --> 00:05:21,327
more eating out.

178
00:05:21,507 --> 00:05:23,469
Each upgrade feels earned,

179
00:05:23,469 --> 00:05:24,223
and it is.

180
00:05:24,403 --> 00:05:26,710
But if your spending rises as fast

181
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:27,727
as your income,

182
00:05:27,727 --> 00:05:29,763
you stay on level two forever,

183
00:05:29,763 --> 00:05:31,323
just with nicer things.

184
00:05:31,672 --> 00:05:33,531
The move to level three is to

185
00:05:33,531 --> 00:05:34,749
pay yourself first.

186
00:05:34,929 --> 00:05:37,271
Set up an automatic transfer of even

187
00:05:37,271 --> 00:05:38,832
fifty dollars on payday,

188
00:05:38,832 --> 00:05:40,914
into a separate savings account,

189
00:05:40,914 --> 00:05:42,476
before you can spend it.

190
00:05:42,656 --> 00:05:44,228
Then, the next time you get a

191
00:05:44,228 --> 00:05:45,909
raise, send at least half of it

192
00:05:45,909 --> 00:05:46,777
to that account.

193
00:05:46,957 --> 00:05:48,887
Your lifestyle still improves,

194
00:05:48,887 --> 00:05:50,881
and your savings finally start.

195
00:05:51,231 --> 00:05:52,137
Level three.

196
00:05:52,317 --> 00:05:53,408
The starter cushion.

197
00:05:53,588 --> 00:05:56,120
You have somewhere between one thousand and

198
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:57,710
ten thousand dollars saved.

199
00:05:57,890 --> 00:05:59,818
A surprise bill is annoying,

200
00:05:59,818 --> 00:06:01,884
but it's not a crisis anymore.

201
00:06:02,233 --> 00:06:03,929
Level three feels different.

202
00:06:04,109 --> 00:06:05,084
The car repair,

203
00:06:05,084 --> 00:06:05,930
the vet bill,

204
00:06:05,930 --> 00:06:07,426
the urgent flight home,

205
00:06:07,426 --> 00:06:08,532
you can pay them.

206
00:06:08,712 --> 00:06:10,062
You don't have to borrow,

207
00:06:10,062 --> 00:06:11,790
and you don't have to ask anyone

208
00:06:11,790 --> 00:06:12,276
for help.

209
00:06:12,456 --> 00:06:14,126
That alone takes a weight off your

210
00:06:14,126 --> 00:06:16,335
shoulders that most people don't even realize

211
00:06:16,335 --> 00:06:17,170
they're carrying.

212
00:06:17,519 --> 00:06:20,027
Here's how close this level really is.

213
00:06:20,207 --> 00:06:22,535
Saving five hundred dollars a month gets

214
00:06:22,535 --> 00:06:24,688
you to ten thousand dollars in twenty

215
00:06:24,688 --> 00:06:25,095
months.

216
00:06:25,275 --> 00:06:27,238
Two hundred fifty a month gets you

217
00:06:27,238 --> 00:06:29,027
there in under three and a half

218
00:06:29,027 --> 00:06:29,373
years.

219
00:06:29,553 --> 00:06:30,645
It isn't magic.

220
00:06:30,825 --> 00:06:32,984
It's just a transfer that happens every

221
00:06:32,984 --> 00:06:35,364
single month without you thinking about it.

222
00:06:35,714 --> 00:06:37,688
The trap at level three is raiding

223
00:06:37,688 --> 00:06:40,417
the cushion for things that aren't emergencies.

224
00:06:40,597 --> 00:06:42,375
A sale, a vacation,

225
00:06:42,375 --> 00:06:43,499
a new phone.

226
00:06:43,679 --> 00:06:46,108
Then the real emergency arrives and the

227
00:06:46,108 --> 00:06:46,918
money's gone.

228
00:06:47,098 --> 00:06:48,523
The fix is to keep it in

229
00:06:48,523 --> 00:06:49,651
a separate account,

230
00:06:49,651 --> 00:06:51,314
ideally at a different bank,

231
00:06:51,314 --> 00:06:53,867
so spending it takes a deliberate decision.

232
00:06:54,216 --> 00:06:55,948
The move to level four is to

233
00:06:55,948 --> 00:06:57,926
grow the cushion to three to six

234
00:06:57,926 --> 00:06:59,534
months of essential costs,

235
00:06:59,534 --> 00:07:01,203
and keep it in a high yield

236
00:07:01,203 --> 00:07:02,193
savings account.

237
00:07:02,373 --> 00:07:03,832
Then, once it's full,

238
00:07:03,832 --> 00:07:06,961
start investing a regular amount every month,

239
00:07:06,961 --> 00:07:08,143
even a small one.

240
00:07:08,323 --> 00:07:10,617
That's the moment your money starts working,

241
00:07:10,617 --> 00:07:11,504
not just sitting.

242
00:07:11,853 --> 00:07:12,933
Quick pause.

243
00:07:13,113 --> 00:07:15,086
If you've already found your level,

244
00:07:15,086 --> 00:07:16,608
I'd love to know which one.

245
00:07:16,788 --> 00:07:18,088
Tell me in the comments.

246
00:07:18,268 --> 00:07:19,757
And if this is helping you,

247
00:07:19,757 --> 00:07:21,136
subscribe to the channel.

248
00:07:21,316 --> 00:07:23,103
It's free, and it helps me keep

249
00:07:23,103 --> 00:07:24,718
making these videos for you.

250
00:07:25,067 --> 00:07:26,008
Level four.

251
00:07:26,188 --> 00:07:27,012
Stable.

252
00:07:27,192 --> 00:07:28,814
Your emergency fund is full,

253
00:07:28,814 --> 00:07:30,668
your high interest debt is gone,

254
00:07:30,668 --> 00:07:32,579
and you're investing every month.

255
00:07:32,759 --> 00:07:35,213
Your net worth is somewhere between ten

256
00:07:35,213 --> 00:07:37,856
thousand and one hundred thousand dollars.

257
00:07:38,205 --> 00:07:39,935
This is where life gets calmer.

258
00:07:40,115 --> 00:07:41,377
A layoff would hurt,

259
00:07:41,377 --> 00:07:43,459
but you'd have months to recover.

260
00:07:43,639 --> 00:07:45,864
You're not checking your balance before every

261
00:07:45,864 --> 00:07:46,309
purchase.

262
00:07:46,489 --> 00:07:48,003
And for the first time,

263
00:07:48,003 --> 00:07:51,160
you're putting money into investments like broad

264
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:53,529
index funds or a retirement account,

265
00:07:53,529 --> 00:07:55,568
on purpose, every single month.

266
00:07:55,919 --> 00:07:56,952
At level four,

267
00:07:56,952 --> 00:07:59,903
your own contributions do almost all the

268
00:07:59,903 --> 00:08:00,272
work.

269
00:08:00,452 --> 00:08:02,573
If you invest three hundred dollars a

270
00:08:02,573 --> 00:08:03,720
month for ten years,

271
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:06,070
at a seven percent average yearly return,

272
00:08:06,070 --> 00:08:08,707
you'd have roughly fifty two thousand dollars.

273
00:08:08,887 --> 00:08:10,791
You put in thirty six thousand.

274
00:08:10,971 --> 00:08:12,042
The rest is growth,

275
00:08:12,042 --> 00:08:13,734
and it's only getting started.

276
00:08:14,084 --> 00:08:16,116
The trap at level four is getting

277
00:08:16,116 --> 00:08:16,732
impatient.

278
00:08:16,912 --> 00:08:18,564
The growth feels slow,

279
00:08:18,564 --> 00:08:20,743
so people chase faster money.

280
00:08:20,923 --> 00:08:22,981
Hot stocks, crypto tips,

281
00:08:22,981 --> 00:08:25,126
a friend's business idea.

282
00:08:25,306 --> 00:08:26,571
Some of it might work.

283
00:08:26,751 --> 00:08:28,609
Most of it costs people years.

284
00:08:28,789 --> 00:08:29,845
At this level,

285
00:08:29,845 --> 00:08:33,543
boring and consistent beats exciting almost every

286
00:08:33,543 --> 00:08:33,920
time.

287
00:08:34,270 --> 00:08:35,783
The move to level five is to

288
00:08:35,783 --> 00:08:37,080
raise your savings rate.

289
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:38,558
Not just your income,

290
00:08:38,558 --> 00:08:39,176
your rate.

291
00:08:39,356 --> 00:08:41,724
Try going from ten percent of your

292
00:08:41,724 --> 00:08:42,978
income to fifteen,

293
00:08:42,978 --> 00:08:43,814
then twenty.

294
00:08:43,994 --> 00:08:45,170
Use every raise,

295
00:08:45,170 --> 00:08:47,815
every bonus, and every paid off loan

296
00:08:47,815 --> 00:08:49,137
to push it higher.

297
00:08:49,317 --> 00:08:50,989
That's what gets you to one hundred

298
00:08:50,989 --> 00:08:51,419
thousand.

299
00:08:51,768 --> 00:08:52,779
Level five.

300
00:08:52,959 --> 00:08:53,725
Momentum.

301
00:08:53,905 --> 00:08:55,918
Your net worth is between one hundred

302
00:08:55,918 --> 00:08:58,584
thousand and about five hundred thousand dollars.

303
00:08:58,764 --> 00:09:00,474
This is where your money starts to

304
00:09:00,474 --> 00:09:01,480
pull its own weight.

305
00:09:01,830 --> 00:09:04,902
Charlie Munger, Warren Buffett's longtime partner,

306
00:09:04,902 --> 00:09:07,604
famously said the first one hundred thousand

307
00:09:07,604 --> 00:09:09,017
dollars is the hardest.

308
00:09:09,197 --> 00:09:10,056
Here's why.

309
00:09:10,236 --> 00:09:11,302
At seven percent,

310
00:09:11,302 --> 00:09:13,811
one hundred thousand dollars can grow by

311
00:09:13,811 --> 00:09:16,382
around seven thousand dollars in a single

312
00:09:16,382 --> 00:09:18,514
year, without you adding anything.

313
00:09:18,694 --> 00:09:20,532
That's a real raise you didn't have

314
00:09:20,532 --> 00:09:21,109
to ask for.

315
00:09:21,458 --> 00:09:22,678
And it compounds.

316
00:09:22,858 --> 00:09:25,764
One hundred thousand dollars plus one thousand

317
00:09:25,764 --> 00:09:26,775
dollars a month,

318
00:09:26,775 --> 00:09:29,366
at a seven percent average yearly return,

319
00:09:29,366 --> 00:09:32,083
reaches one million dollars in about twenty

320
00:09:32,083 --> 00:09:32,714
one years.

321
00:09:32,894 --> 00:09:34,269
That isn't a promise,

322
00:09:34,269 --> 00:09:36,626
markets vary, but it shows how level

323
00:09:36,626 --> 00:09:39,245
five turns into level six with patience.

324
00:09:39,594 --> 00:09:41,779
The trap at level five is fees

325
00:09:41,779 --> 00:09:42,799
and lifestyle.

326
00:09:42,979 --> 00:09:45,247
A one percent yearly fee doesn't sound

327
00:09:45,247 --> 00:09:47,455
like much, but on a growing portfolio

328
00:09:47,455 --> 00:09:49,604
it can cost hundreds of thousands of

329
00:09:49,604 --> 00:09:51,036
dollars over a lifetime.

330
00:09:51,216 --> 00:09:53,242
And this is the level where people

331
00:09:53,242 --> 00:09:54,911
upgrade their house and car,

332
00:09:54,911 --> 00:09:57,176
and lock in costs that slow everything

333
00:09:57,176 --> 00:09:57,474
down.

334
00:09:57,823 --> 00:09:59,584
The move to level six is to

335
00:09:59,584 --> 00:10:00,888
protect the machine.

336
00:10:01,068 --> 00:10:02,331
Keep your fees low,

337
00:10:02,331 --> 00:10:04,724
keep investing through market drops,

338
00:10:04,724 --> 00:10:07,050
and keep your big fixed costs under

339
00:10:07,050 --> 00:10:07,582
control.

340
00:10:07,762 --> 00:10:09,975
Most people who reach level six didn't

341
00:10:09,975 --> 00:10:11,256
do anything brilliant.

342
00:10:11,436 --> 00:10:13,480
They just didn't interrupt the process.

343
00:10:13,829 --> 00:10:14,851
Level six.

344
00:10:15,031 --> 00:10:15,960
Millionaire.

345
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:18,651
Your net worth has passed one million

346
00:10:18,651 --> 00:10:19,193
dollars.

347
00:10:19,373 --> 00:10:20,917
It sounds like the finish line.

348
00:10:21,097 --> 00:10:23,047
For most people who get here,

349
00:10:23,047 --> 00:10:23,652
it isn't.

350
00:10:24,001 --> 00:10:26,882
UBS's Global Wealth Report estimates there are

351
00:10:26,882 --> 00:10:29,638
more than twenty million millionaires in the

352
00:10:29,638 --> 00:10:30,515
United States.

353
00:10:30,695 --> 00:10:32,564
Most of them are not celebrities.

354
00:10:32,744 --> 00:10:35,299
They're often people in their fifties and

355
00:10:35,299 --> 00:10:37,605
sixties who owned a home for decades,

356
00:10:37,605 --> 00:10:39,412
saved in retirement accounts,

357
00:10:39,412 --> 00:10:40,534
and never stopped.

358
00:10:40,714 --> 00:10:43,280
Many of them drive ordinary cars.

359
00:10:43,629 --> 00:10:45,197
Here's the reality check.

360
00:10:45,377 --> 00:10:47,234
One million dollars sounds rich,

361
00:10:47,234 --> 00:10:48,686
but if half of it is your

362
00:10:48,686 --> 00:10:50,950
home, the money you could actually live

363
00:10:50,950 --> 00:10:52,053
on is much smaller.

364
00:10:52,233 --> 00:10:54,434
Using the four percent rule of thumb,

365
00:10:54,434 --> 00:10:57,589
five hundred thousand dollars in investments supports

366
00:10:57,589 --> 00:10:59,791
about twenty thousand dollars a year.

367
00:10:59,971 --> 00:11:01,805
Nice, but not freedom yet.

368
00:11:02,154 --> 00:11:04,131
The trap at level six is feeling

369
00:11:04,131 --> 00:11:05,614
rich and acting like it.

370
00:11:05,794 --> 00:11:07,804
Bigger house, second home,

371
00:11:07,804 --> 00:11:09,196
expensive hobbies.

372
00:11:09,376 --> 00:11:10,410
At this level,

373
00:11:10,410 --> 00:11:13,219
a few large decisions can easily erase

374
00:11:13,219 --> 00:11:14,844
ten years of progress.

375
00:11:15,024 --> 00:11:16,982
The people who stay here keep their

376
00:11:16,982 --> 00:11:19,387
spending tied to what their investments can

377
00:11:19,387 --> 00:11:20,225
really support.

378
00:11:20,575 --> 00:11:22,348
The move to level seven is to

379
00:11:22,348 --> 00:11:25,037
stop measuring net worth and start measuring

380
00:11:25,037 --> 00:11:27,054
one thing: how many years of your

381
00:11:27,054 --> 00:11:29,132
life your investments can pay for.

382
00:11:29,312 --> 00:11:31,239
That changes every decision.

383
00:11:31,419 --> 00:11:34,391
Suddenly a cheaper house isn't a sacrifice.

384
00:11:34,571 --> 00:11:36,522
It's years of your freedom bought back.

385
00:11:36,871 --> 00:11:38,009
Level seven.

386
00:11:38,189 --> 00:11:39,849
Financially independent.

387
00:11:40,029 --> 00:11:42,311
Your investments can pay for your life,

388
00:11:42,311 --> 00:11:43,071
indefinitely.

389
00:11:43,251 --> 00:11:44,701
Work becomes a choice,

390
00:11:44,701 --> 00:11:45,887
not a requirement.

391
00:11:46,236 --> 00:11:48,282
A common rule of thumb comes from

392
00:11:48,282 --> 00:11:51,196
research by financial planner William Bengen in

393
00:11:51,196 --> 00:11:52,560
the nineteen nineties:

394
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:55,226
withdrawing about four percent in the first

395
00:11:55,226 --> 00:11:57,396
year, then adjusting for inflation,

396
00:11:57,396 --> 00:12:00,186
has historically lasted at least thirty years

397
00:12:00,186 --> 00:12:01,178
in most periods.

398
00:12:01,358 --> 00:12:02,335
Flip that around,

399
00:12:02,335 --> 00:12:04,402
and you need about twenty five times

400
00:12:04,402 --> 00:12:05,608
your yearly spending.

401
00:12:05,957 --> 00:12:07,792
So the number is personal.

402
00:12:07,972 --> 00:12:10,230
If you spend forty thousand dollars a

403
00:12:10,230 --> 00:12:12,670
year, you need about one million dollars

404
00:12:12,670 --> 00:12:13,220
invested.

405
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:15,209
If you spend eighty thousand,

406
00:12:15,209 --> 00:12:16,894
you need about two million.

407
00:12:17,074 --> 00:12:19,826
That's why spending less is so powerful.

408
00:12:20,006 --> 00:12:21,788
It lowers the finish line and gets

409
00:12:21,788 --> 00:12:23,570
you there faster at the same time.

410
00:12:23,919 --> 00:12:26,254
The trap at level seven is assuming

411
00:12:26,254 --> 00:12:27,855
the rule is a guarantee.

412
00:12:28,035 --> 00:12:28,442
It isn't.

413
00:12:28,622 --> 00:12:30,763
A bad market right at the start

414
00:12:30,763 --> 00:12:32,835
of retirement, high inflation,

415
00:12:32,835 --> 00:12:35,184
or big health costs can change the

416
00:12:35,184 --> 00:12:35,529
math.

417
00:12:35,709 --> 00:12:37,922
Most people at this level keep some

418
00:12:37,922 --> 00:12:40,640
flexibility, like spending a little less in

419
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:42,536
bad years, or earning a bit on

420
00:12:42,536 --> 00:12:43,105
the side.

421
00:12:43,455 --> 00:12:45,730
And here's what people at level seven

422
00:12:45,730 --> 00:12:47,205
say surprised them most.

423
00:12:47,385 --> 00:12:48,396
It wasn't the money.

424
00:12:48,576 --> 00:12:49,620
It was the time.

425
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:50,861
Time with family,

426
00:12:50,861 --> 00:12:53,357
time to build something they care about,

427
00:12:53,357 --> 00:12:54,293
time to say no.

428
00:12:54,473 --> 00:12:57,140
That's what all seven levels are really

429
00:12:57,140 --> 00:12:57,550
about.

430
00:12:57,730 --> 00:12:58,879
Not getting rich.

431
00:12:59,059 --> 00:12:59,988
Getting free.

432
00:13:00,338 --> 00:13:02,265
Now let's look at these seven levels

433
00:13:02,265 --> 00:13:03,763
from three different angles.

434
00:13:03,943 --> 00:13:05,100
First the numbers,

435
00:13:05,100 --> 00:13:06,386
then the psychology,

436
00:13:06,386 --> 00:13:07,542
then the playbook.

437
00:13:07,722 --> 00:13:10,091
Because knowing your level is one thing.

438
00:13:10,271 --> 00:13:12,679
Understanding why people get stuck there,

439
00:13:12,679 --> 00:13:14,558
and exactly what to do about it,

440
00:13:14,558 --> 00:13:16,320
is what actually moves you up.

441
00:13:16,669 --> 00:13:17,494
Angle one.

442
00:13:17,674 --> 00:13:18,289
The numbers.

443
00:13:18,469 --> 00:13:20,982
The biggest number on every level isn't

444
00:13:20,982 --> 00:13:21,755
your salary.

445
00:13:21,935 --> 00:13:24,060
It's the gap between what comes in

446
00:13:24,060 --> 00:13:25,185
and what goes out.

447
00:13:25,365 --> 00:13:27,537
Economists call it your savings rate.

448
00:13:27,717 --> 00:13:29,918
If you earn four thousand dollars a

449
00:13:29,918 --> 00:13:32,433
month after tax and spend three thousand

450
00:13:32,433 --> 00:13:34,760
six hundred, your savings rate is ten

451
00:13:34,760 --> 00:13:35,263
percent.

452
00:13:35,443 --> 00:13:38,005
If you spend three thousand two hundred,

453
00:13:38,005 --> 00:13:39,286
it's twenty percent.

454
00:13:39,466 --> 00:13:41,643
That one number decides how fast you

455
00:13:41,643 --> 00:13:44,062
climb, more than any investment you will

456
00:13:44,062 --> 00:13:44,667
ever pick.

457
00:13:45,017 --> 00:13:46,829
Here's what the gap does over time.

458
00:13:47,009 --> 00:13:48,814
Two hundred dollars a month,

459
00:13:48,814 --> 00:13:51,135
invested for thirty years at a seven

460
00:13:51,135 --> 00:13:53,069
percent average yearly return,

461
00:13:53,069 --> 00:13:55,584
grows to roughly two hundred forty four

462
00:13:55,584 --> 00:13:56,680
thousand dollars.

463
00:13:56,860 --> 00:13:58,722
Double it to four hundred a month,

464
00:13:58,722 --> 00:14:01,022
and it's roughly four hundred eighty eight

465
00:14:01,022 --> 00:14:01,515
thousand.

466
00:14:01,695 --> 00:14:03,623
Same person, same market.

467
00:14:03,803 --> 00:14:05,498
The only difference is the gap.

468
00:14:05,847 --> 00:14:07,868
Now look at the math of debt,

469
00:14:07,868 --> 00:14:10,375
because it works in exactly the same

470
00:14:10,375 --> 00:14:11,769
way, just backwards.

471
00:14:11,949 --> 00:14:14,595
A five thousand dollar credit card balance

472
00:14:14,595 --> 00:14:17,115
at twenty two percent costs about eleven

473
00:14:17,115 --> 00:14:19,321
hundred dollars a year in interest.

474
00:14:19,501 --> 00:14:21,832
That's eleven hundred dollars that could have

475
00:14:21,832 --> 00:14:23,748
been invested, and instead it went to

476
00:14:23,748 --> 00:14:24,215
the bank.

477
00:14:24,395 --> 00:14:25,263
On level one,

478
00:14:25,263 --> 00:14:28,338
compound interest is working against you every

479
00:14:28,338 --> 00:14:29,073
single day.

480
00:14:29,253 --> 00:14:31,484
The first goal is simply to switch

481
00:14:31,484 --> 00:14:31,877
sides.

482
00:14:32,227 --> 00:14:35,013
Here's another number that matters on levels

483
00:14:35,013 --> 00:14:35,900
two and three:

484
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:36,976
months of runway.

485
00:14:37,156 --> 00:14:38,997
Take the money you could reach in

486
00:14:38,997 --> 00:14:40,614
a week, and divide it by what

487
00:14:40,614 --> 00:14:42,566
you spend in a month on essentials.

488
00:14:42,746 --> 00:14:44,876
Rent, food, transport,

489
00:14:44,876 --> 00:14:47,588
insurance, minimum payments.

490
00:14:47,768 --> 00:14:49,506
If the answer is under one,

491
00:14:49,506 --> 00:14:51,181
any surprise becomes debt.

492
00:14:51,361 --> 00:14:52,962
Between one and three,

493
00:14:52,962 --> 00:14:55,146
you can absorb most surprises.

494
00:14:55,326 --> 00:14:56,645
Over three to six,

495
00:14:56,645 --> 00:14:58,845
a job loss becomes a hard time

496
00:14:58,845 --> 00:15:00,457
instead of a disaster.

497
00:15:00,807 --> 00:15:02,236
On levels four and five,

498
00:15:02,236 --> 00:15:04,319
the number that starts to matter is

499
00:15:04,319 --> 00:15:06,462
the one most people never calculate:

500
00:15:06,462 --> 00:15:08,962
what their money earned this year compared

501
00:15:08,962 --> 00:15:10,212
with what they added.

502
00:15:10,392 --> 00:15:11,673
In the early years,

503
00:15:11,673 --> 00:15:13,833
almost all of the growth is your

504
00:15:13,833 --> 00:15:15,047
own contributions.

505
00:15:15,227 --> 00:15:17,636
But somewhere around the middle of level

506
00:15:17,636 --> 00:15:19,923
five, at a seven percent illustration,

507
00:15:19,923 --> 00:15:21,971
a typical year of growth can match

508
00:15:21,971 --> 00:15:22,994
a year of saving.

509
00:15:23,174 --> 00:15:24,599
That's the crossover point,

510
00:15:24,599 --> 00:15:25,970
and you can see it on your

511
00:15:25,970 --> 00:15:26,762
own statements.

512
00:15:27,111 --> 00:15:28,957
And on levels six and seven,

513
00:15:28,957 --> 00:15:31,001
the number is years of freedom.

514
00:15:31,181 --> 00:15:33,770
Divide your investments by your yearly spending.

515
00:15:33,950 --> 00:15:37,135
Five hundred thousand dollars invested and forty

516
00:15:37,135 --> 00:15:39,590
thousand a year in spending is twelve

517
00:15:39,590 --> 00:15:40,718
and a half years.

518
00:15:40,898 --> 00:15:43,116
One million at the same spending is

519
00:15:43,116 --> 00:15:44,257
twenty five years,

520
00:15:44,257 --> 00:15:46,412
which is the old four percent rule

521
00:15:46,412 --> 00:15:46,982
of thumb.

522
00:15:47,162 --> 00:15:48,711
It's not a perfect measure,

523
00:15:48,711 --> 00:15:50,375
but it's the most honest one,

524
00:15:50,375 --> 00:15:52,325
because it ties your money to your

525
00:15:52,325 --> 00:15:53,013
actual life.

526
00:15:53,363 --> 00:15:55,294
Put those together and you get a

527
00:15:55,294 --> 00:15:57,407
simple scorecard you can fill in on

528
00:15:57,407 --> 00:15:58,553
one piece of paper.

529
00:15:58,733 --> 00:15:59,975
Your savings rate.

530
00:16:00,155 --> 00:16:01,386
Your months of runway.

531
00:16:01,566 --> 00:16:04,260
Your growth compared with your contributions.

532
00:16:04,440 --> 00:16:05,717
And your years of freedom.

533
00:16:05,897 --> 00:16:08,305
Those four numbers tell you your level

534
00:16:08,305 --> 00:16:10,714
more accurately than any salary or any

535
00:16:10,714 --> 00:16:12,236
bank balance ever could.

536
00:16:12,416 --> 00:16:13,598
And the good news is,

537
00:16:13,598 --> 00:16:15,457
every one of them can be improved

538
00:16:15,457 --> 00:16:16,584
starting this month.

539
00:16:16,933 --> 00:16:17,874
Angle two.

540
00:16:18,054 --> 00:16:19,087
The psychology.

541
00:16:19,267 --> 00:16:21,359
Because if money were only math,

542
00:16:21,359 --> 00:16:23,516
everybody would be on level five.

543
00:16:23,696 --> 00:16:24,080
It isn't.

544
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:25,923
Every level has its own way of

545
00:16:25,923 --> 00:16:27,919
thinking, and the way of thinking is

546
00:16:27,919 --> 00:16:29,693
usually what keeps people stuck.

547
00:16:30,042 --> 00:16:30,862
On level one,

548
00:16:30,862 --> 00:16:32,187
the feeling is shame,

549
00:16:32,187 --> 00:16:34,268
and shame makes people look away.

550
00:16:34,448 --> 00:16:36,318
They stop opening statements.

551
00:16:36,498 --> 00:16:38,564
They don't know their exact balances.

552
00:16:38,744 --> 00:16:40,289
And what you don't look at,

553
00:16:40,289 --> 00:16:41,089
you can't fix.

554
00:16:41,269 --> 00:16:44,878
Researchers Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir wrote

555
00:16:44,878 --> 00:16:47,109
about this in their book Scarcity.

556
00:16:47,289 --> 00:16:48,792
When money is very tight,

557
00:16:48,792 --> 00:16:51,258
your mind narrows onto the urgent problem

558
00:16:51,258 --> 00:16:52,220
in front of you,

559
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:54,685
and long term planning gets squeezed out.

560
00:16:54,865 --> 00:16:56,270
It's not a character flaw.

561
00:16:56,450 --> 00:16:58,400
It's what scarcity does to anyone.

562
00:16:58,750 --> 00:17:00,501
So the fix on level one is

563
00:17:00,501 --> 00:17:01,444
not willpower.

564
00:17:01,624 --> 00:17:03,865
It's making the problem small and visible.

565
00:17:04,045 --> 00:17:04,834
One list.

566
00:17:05,014 --> 00:17:06,907
Every debt, every balance,

567
00:17:06,907 --> 00:17:07,708
every rate.

568
00:17:07,888 --> 00:17:10,730
People are often surprised that once the

569
00:17:10,730 --> 00:17:12,934
list exists, the anxiety drops,

570
00:17:12,934 --> 00:17:15,492
even before a single dollar is paid.

571
00:17:15,672 --> 00:17:17,596
The monster under the bed is always

572
00:17:17,596 --> 00:17:19,411
bigger than the monster on paper.

573
00:17:19,760 --> 00:17:20,743
On level two,

574
00:17:20,743 --> 00:17:21,877
the mindset is,

575
00:17:21,877 --> 00:17:22,860
I deserve it.

576
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:24,991
And honestly, you probably do.

577
00:17:25,171 --> 00:17:26,100
You work hard.

578
00:17:26,280 --> 00:17:28,615
But the problem is that every purchase

579
00:17:28,615 --> 00:17:30,029
gets judged on its own,

580
00:17:30,029 --> 00:17:32,979
and almost any single purchase looks reasonable.

581
00:17:33,159 --> 00:17:35,120
The coffee, the delivery,

582
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:36,061
the upgrade.

583
00:17:36,241 --> 00:17:39,933
Behavioral economists call this mental accounting.

584
00:17:40,113 --> 00:17:42,409
We put money in different mental buckets,

585
00:17:42,409 --> 00:17:43,921
and we don't see the total.

586
00:17:44,101 --> 00:17:46,650
Level two people rarely have one big

587
00:17:46,650 --> 00:17:47,004
leak.

588
00:17:47,184 --> 00:17:48,728
They have thirty small ones.

589
00:17:49,077 --> 00:17:50,129
On level three,

590
00:17:50,129 --> 00:17:52,161
the feeling shifts to relief,

591
00:17:52,161 --> 00:17:54,194
and relief brings a new risk:

592
00:17:54,194 --> 00:17:54,755
comfort.

593
00:17:54,935 --> 00:17:56,779
You've got a few thousand dollars,

594
00:17:56,779 --> 00:17:57,809
the pressure's off,

595
00:17:57,809 --> 00:17:59,219
and it's tempting to stop.

596
00:17:59,399 --> 00:18:01,486
This is the level where many people

597
00:18:01,486 --> 00:18:02,441
stall for years.

598
00:18:02,621 --> 00:18:04,737
Not because they can't save more,

599
00:18:04,737 --> 00:18:06,789
but because the urgency is gone.

600
00:18:06,969 --> 00:18:08,951
The trick is to replace fear with

601
00:18:08,951 --> 00:18:09,372
a goal.

602
00:18:09,552 --> 00:18:10,887
Not, I need money.

603
00:18:11,067 --> 00:18:13,168
But, I want ten thousand dollars by

604
00:18:13,168 --> 00:18:13,888
next summer.

605
00:18:14,238 --> 00:18:15,079
On level four,

606
00:18:15,079 --> 00:18:16,340
the enemy is boredom.

607
00:18:16,520 --> 00:18:19,113
Investing every month in a broad fund

608
00:18:19,113 --> 00:18:20,235
is not exciting.

609
00:18:20,415 --> 00:18:22,110
Nothing happens most days.

610
00:18:22,290 --> 00:18:24,159
So people start tinkering.

611
00:18:24,339 --> 00:18:25,856
They check prices daily,

612
00:18:25,856 --> 00:18:27,309
they move money around,

613
00:18:27,309 --> 00:18:29,773
they chase whatever went up last month.

614
00:18:29,953 --> 00:18:31,834
Studies of investor behavior,

615
00:18:31,834 --> 00:18:34,234
like the long running research by the

616
00:18:34,234 --> 00:18:37,023
firm DALBAR, have repeatedly found that the

617
00:18:37,023 --> 00:18:39,747
average investor earns less than the funds

618
00:18:39,747 --> 00:18:40,720
they invest in,

619
00:18:40,720 --> 00:18:42,666
largely because of bad timing.

620
00:18:43,015 --> 00:18:44,019
On level five,

621
00:18:44,019 --> 00:18:45,883
the mindset is comparison.

622
00:18:46,063 --> 00:18:48,292
Your friends upgrade their houses.

623
00:18:48,472 --> 00:18:50,458
Your coworker buys a new truck.

624
00:18:50,638 --> 00:18:53,033
Social media shows you people your age

625
00:18:53,033 --> 00:18:54,608
on beaches every weekend.

626
00:18:54,788 --> 00:18:57,004
And because you now have real money,

627
00:18:57,004 --> 00:18:58,604
you can afford to keep up,

628
00:18:58,604 --> 00:18:59,897
at least for a while.

629
00:19:00,077 --> 00:19:03,490
Psychologists sometimes call this the hedonic treadmill.

630
00:19:03,670 --> 00:19:05,816
Every upgrade feels great for a few

631
00:19:05,816 --> 00:19:07,718
months, then it becomes normal,

632
00:19:07,718 --> 00:19:09,312
and you need the next one.

633
00:19:09,662 --> 00:19:10,647
On level six,

634
00:19:10,647 --> 00:19:12,693
the risk is overconfidence.

635
00:19:12,873 --> 00:19:13,832
You did it once,

636
00:19:13,832 --> 00:19:16,112
so you start believing you can't lose.

637
00:19:16,292 --> 00:19:18,170
People take bigger bets,

638
00:19:18,170 --> 00:19:20,596
put too much money in one stock

639
00:19:20,596 --> 00:19:21,535
or one deal,

640
00:19:21,535 --> 00:19:23,961
or lend to someone with a great

641
00:19:23,961 --> 00:19:24,430
story.

642
00:19:24,610 --> 00:19:25,855
And on level seven,

643
00:19:25,855 --> 00:19:28,279
the surprising feeling is often fear.

644
00:19:28,459 --> 00:19:30,981
People who saved for decades find it

645
00:19:30,981 --> 00:19:32,592
hard to actually spend.

646
00:19:32,772 --> 00:19:35,234
Some keep working long after they need

647
00:19:35,234 --> 00:19:37,695
to, just because spending feels wrong.

648
00:19:38,044 --> 00:19:39,577
So here's the pattern.

649
00:19:39,757 --> 00:19:41,753
Shame, then entitlement,

650
00:19:41,753 --> 00:19:43,583
then comfort, boredom,

651
00:19:43,583 --> 00:19:47,327
comparison, overconfidence, and finally fear.

652
00:19:47,507 --> 00:19:49,656
Every level has a feeling that pulls

653
00:19:49,656 --> 00:19:50,432
you sideways.

654
00:19:50,612 --> 00:19:52,529
If you know which feeling belongs to

655
00:19:52,529 --> 00:19:54,339
your level, you can see it coming.

656
00:19:54,519 --> 00:19:56,129
And seeing it coming is half the

657
00:19:56,129 --> 00:19:56,481
battle.

658
00:19:56,831 --> 00:19:57,853
Angle three.

659
00:19:58,033 --> 00:19:58,776
The playbook.

660
00:19:58,956 --> 00:20:00,879
Here's exactly what I'd do this week

661
00:20:00,879 --> 00:20:01,626
on each level.

662
00:20:01,806 --> 00:20:02,700
Not someday.

663
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:03,461
This week.

664
00:20:03,641 --> 00:20:05,451
Find yours and do just the steps

665
00:20:05,451 --> 00:20:06,300
for your level.

666
00:20:06,649 --> 00:20:08,112
Level one, this week.

667
00:20:08,292 --> 00:20:09,977
Write the list of every debt with

668
00:20:09,977 --> 00:20:11,508
its balance and interest rate.

669
00:20:11,688 --> 00:20:14,813
Cancel or pause any subscription you didn't

670
00:20:14,813 --> 00:20:15,903
use last month.

671
00:20:16,083 --> 00:20:18,585
Call your credit card company and ask

672
00:20:18,585 --> 00:20:20,344
for a lower interest rate.

673
00:20:20,524 --> 00:20:21,958
It doesn't always work,

674
00:20:21,958 --> 00:20:23,705
but it costs nothing to ask.

675
00:20:23,885 --> 00:20:25,150
And pick your target:

676
00:20:25,150 --> 00:20:26,476
the highest rate debt,

677
00:20:26,476 --> 00:20:28,766
which gets every extra dollar from now

678
00:20:28,766 --> 00:20:28,947
on.

679
00:20:29,296 --> 00:20:30,968
Level two, this week.

680
00:20:31,148 --> 00:20:32,934
Open a separate savings account,

681
00:20:32,934 --> 00:20:35,000
ideally at a different bank from your

682
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:35,502
checking.

683
00:20:35,682 --> 00:20:38,303
Set an automatic transfer on payday,

684
00:20:38,303 --> 00:20:40,779
even twenty five or fifty dollars.

685
00:20:40,959 --> 00:20:42,576
Then look at your last month of

686
00:20:42,576 --> 00:20:44,872
spending and circle every purchase you don't

687
00:20:44,872 --> 00:20:45,707
remember making.

688
00:20:45,887 --> 00:20:47,130
Those are your leaks.

689
00:20:47,310 --> 00:20:48,587
Pick three to stop.

690
00:20:48,936 --> 00:20:50,573
Level three, this week.

691
00:20:50,753 --> 00:20:53,536
Write down your essential monthly costs and

692
00:20:53,536 --> 00:20:54,701
multiply by three.

693
00:20:54,881 --> 00:20:55,891
That's your next target.

694
00:20:56,071 --> 00:20:58,145
Move your cushion to a high yield

695
00:20:58,145 --> 00:21:01,098
savings account if it's earning almost nothing.

696
00:21:01,278 --> 00:21:03,946
Rates change, so compare a few banks,

697
00:21:03,946 --> 00:21:06,181
and check that the bank is FDIC

698
00:21:06,181 --> 00:21:06,758
insured.

699
00:21:06,938 --> 00:21:08,641
And give the account a name,

700
00:21:08,641 --> 00:21:09,736
like Freedom Fund.

701
00:21:09,916 --> 00:21:10,958
It sounds silly,

702
00:21:10,958 --> 00:21:13,236
but named goals are harder to raid.

703
00:21:13,586 --> 00:21:15,340
Level four, this week.

704
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:17,848
If your employer offers a retirement plan

705
00:21:17,848 --> 00:21:18,587
with a match,

706
00:21:18,587 --> 00:21:21,143
make sure you're contributing at least enough

707
00:21:21,143 --> 00:21:22,393
to get the full match.

708
00:21:22,573 --> 00:21:24,193
That's part of your pay you're leaving

709
00:21:24,193 --> 00:21:25,173
on the table otherwise.

710
00:21:25,353 --> 00:21:27,806
Then set your investing on autopilot,

711
00:21:27,806 --> 00:21:30,392
and turn off daily price notifications.

712
00:21:30,572 --> 00:21:32,719
Check your investments once a month,

713
00:21:32,719 --> 00:21:33,196
at most.

714
00:21:33,545 --> 00:21:35,275
Level five, this week.

715
00:21:35,455 --> 00:21:37,805
Find the expense ratio of every fund

716
00:21:37,805 --> 00:21:39,566
you own and add up what you

717
00:21:39,566 --> 00:21:41,133
pay in dollars per year.

718
00:21:41,313 --> 00:21:44,308
Then write down your three biggest fixed

719
00:21:44,308 --> 00:21:45,881
costs: housing, cars,

720
00:21:45,881 --> 00:21:46,630
insurance.

721
00:21:46,810 --> 00:21:49,101
Before any upgrade to one of them,

722
00:21:49,101 --> 00:21:50,583
ask a simple question.

723
00:21:50,763 --> 00:21:52,796
How many months of freedom does this

724
00:21:52,796 --> 00:21:53,248
cost me?

725
00:21:53,428 --> 00:21:54,914
Sometimes it's worth it.

726
00:21:55,094 --> 00:21:56,057
Often it isn't.

727
00:21:56,407 --> 00:21:57,963
Level six, this week.

728
00:21:58,143 --> 00:21:59,811
Check that no single stock,

729
00:21:59,811 --> 00:22:02,096
property or business is so large that

730
00:22:02,096 --> 00:22:04,320
losing it would drop you two levels.

731
00:22:04,500 --> 00:22:06,432
Review your insurance and your will,

732
00:22:06,432 --> 00:22:08,042
or start one if you don't have

733
00:22:08,042 --> 00:22:08,203
it.

734
00:22:08,383 --> 00:22:09,519
And level seven,

735
00:22:09,519 --> 00:22:10,229
this week.

736
00:22:10,409 --> 00:22:12,747
Write a simple spending plan for the

737
00:22:12,747 --> 00:22:13,981
next twelve months,

738
00:22:13,981 --> 00:22:16,319
including a version for a bad market

739
00:22:16,319 --> 00:22:16,644
year.

740
00:22:16,824 --> 00:22:18,973
Then, and this is the hard part,

741
00:22:18,973 --> 00:22:21,189
plan something to actually enjoy.

742
00:22:21,539 --> 00:22:23,281
Notice something about this playbook.

743
00:22:23,461 --> 00:22:25,597
None of it requires a high income.

744
00:22:25,777 --> 00:22:28,020
None of it requires predicting the stock

745
00:22:28,020 --> 00:22:28,413
market.

746
00:22:28,593 --> 00:22:31,090
It's lists, automatic transfers,

747
00:22:31,090 --> 00:22:33,198
a few phone calls and a few

748
00:22:33,198 --> 00:22:33,666
rules.

749
00:22:33,846 --> 00:22:36,458
That's the honest truth about money levels.

750
00:22:36,638 --> 00:22:38,448
The people at the top mostly did

751
00:22:38,448 --> 00:22:40,145
simple things for a long time.

752
00:22:40,494 --> 00:22:42,392
So how long does it actually take

753
00:22:42,392 --> 00:22:42,909
to climb?

754
00:22:43,089 --> 00:22:44,387
Let me show you the math,

755
00:22:44,387 --> 00:22:46,723
because it's more encouraging than you think.

756
00:22:47,073 --> 00:22:48,304
Here's one path.

757
00:22:48,484 --> 00:22:50,343
Five hundred dollars a month,

758
00:22:50,343 --> 00:22:52,650
invested for thirty years at a seven

759
00:22:52,650 --> 00:22:54,573
percent average yearly return,

760
00:22:54,573 --> 00:22:57,201
grows to roughly six hundred ten thousand

761
00:22:57,201 --> 00:22:57,714
dollars.

762
00:22:57,894 --> 00:23:00,216
You'd put in one hundred eighty thousand.

763
00:23:00,396 --> 00:23:02,120
More than two thirds of the final

764
00:23:02,120 --> 00:23:03,008
number is growth.

765
00:23:03,358 --> 00:23:04,543
Now double the effort.

766
00:23:04,723 --> 00:23:07,089
One thousand dollars a month for twenty

767
00:23:07,089 --> 00:23:09,335
five years, at the same seven percent

768
00:23:09,335 --> 00:23:12,248
illustration, grows to roughly eight hundred ten

769
00:23:12,248 --> 00:23:13,279
thousand dollars.

770
00:23:13,459 --> 00:23:15,712
You'd put in three hundred thousand.

771
00:23:15,892 --> 00:23:19,119
Higher contributions shorten the climb by years.

772
00:23:19,469 --> 00:23:21,501
The pattern is always the same.

773
00:23:21,681 --> 00:23:24,166
The first levels are about behavior:

774
00:23:24,166 --> 00:23:26,581
stopping debt, saving on autopilot,

775
00:23:26,581 --> 00:23:27,893
building a cushion.

776
00:23:28,073 --> 00:23:30,524
The middle levels are about consistency:

777
00:23:30,524 --> 00:23:33,343
investing every month and not interrupting it.

778
00:23:33,523 --> 00:23:36,254
And the top levels are about protection:

779
00:23:36,254 --> 00:23:38,302
low fees, controlled spending,

780
00:23:38,302 --> 00:23:39,189
and patience.

781
00:23:39,540 --> 00:23:41,235
And here's the part people forget.

782
00:23:41,415 --> 00:23:43,234
You don't have to climb all seven

783
00:23:43,234 --> 00:23:44,723
levels to change your life.

784
00:23:44,903 --> 00:23:47,266
Going from level one to level three,

785
00:23:47,266 --> 00:23:49,104
from debt to a real cushion,

786
00:23:49,104 --> 00:23:51,139
is the biggest jump in peace of

787
00:23:51,139 --> 00:23:53,239
mind most people will ever feel.

788
00:23:53,419 --> 00:23:54,186
Start there.

789
00:23:54,536 --> 00:23:56,580
Numbers are easier to feel when they

790
00:23:56,580 --> 00:23:57,659
belong to a person.

791
00:23:57,839 --> 00:24:00,358
So here are three example climbs.

792
00:24:00,538 --> 00:24:02,197
These are made up examples,

793
00:24:02,197 --> 00:24:03,180
not real people,

794
00:24:03,180 --> 00:24:05,269
but every number in them is simple

795
00:24:05,269 --> 00:24:06,436
math you can check.

796
00:24:06,786 --> 00:24:07,970
Example one.

797
00:24:08,150 --> 00:24:09,487
Maria is twenty six,

798
00:24:09,487 --> 00:24:12,295
earns forty eight thousand dollars a year,

799
00:24:12,295 --> 00:24:14,769
and owes nine thousand dollars on two

800
00:24:14,769 --> 00:24:15,638
credit cards.

801
00:24:15,818 --> 00:24:16,979
She's on level one.

802
00:24:17,159 --> 00:24:18,403
She writes her list,

803
00:24:18,403 --> 00:24:19,771
stops using the cards,

804
00:24:19,771 --> 00:24:22,197
and finds three hundred fifty dollars a

805
00:24:22,197 --> 00:24:24,934
month by cutting delivery food and switching

806
00:24:24,934 --> 00:24:25,867
her phone plan.

807
00:24:26,047 --> 00:24:28,520
Her minimum payments were about two hundred

808
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:30,879
fifty dollars, so now six hundred dollars

809
00:24:30,879 --> 00:24:32,317
a month goes at the debt,

810
00:24:32,317 --> 00:24:33,698
highest rate card first.

811
00:24:33,878 --> 00:24:36,365
At around twenty two percent interest,

812
00:24:36,365 --> 00:24:38,394
she's debt free in about a year

813
00:24:38,394 --> 00:24:39,114
and a half.

814
00:24:39,464 --> 00:24:40,926
Then the magic part.

815
00:24:41,106 --> 00:24:43,164
The moment her last card is paid,

816
00:24:43,164 --> 00:24:44,973
she doesn't spend that money.

817
00:24:45,153 --> 00:24:47,260
She keeps the same payment going,

818
00:24:47,260 --> 00:24:49,622
but now into her own savings account.

819
00:24:49,802 --> 00:24:50,791
In about a year,

820
00:24:50,791 --> 00:24:52,831
she has a cushion of around seven

821
00:24:52,831 --> 00:24:55,302
thousand dollars, with a bit of interest

822
00:24:55,302 --> 00:24:55,735
on top.

823
00:24:55,915 --> 00:24:56,939
At twenty eight,

824
00:24:56,939 --> 00:24:58,283
she's on level three.

825
00:24:58,463 --> 00:24:59,694
Same salary.

826
00:24:59,874 --> 00:25:00,942
Same apartment.

827
00:25:01,122 --> 00:25:02,759
Completely different life.

828
00:25:03,110 --> 00:25:04,224
Example two.

829
00:25:04,404 --> 00:25:06,539
James and Tasha are thirty five,

830
00:25:06,539 --> 00:25:08,875
with two kids and a combined income

831
00:25:08,875 --> 00:25:11,010
of ninety five thousand dollars.

832
00:25:11,190 --> 00:25:12,548
They're on level two:

833
00:25:12,548 --> 00:25:14,812
no debt except the mortgage and one

834
00:25:14,812 --> 00:25:16,946
car loan, but nothing left at the

835
00:25:16,946 --> 00:25:18,110
end of each month.

836
00:25:18,290 --> 00:25:19,649
When the car loan ends,

837
00:25:19,649 --> 00:25:21,895
they keep driving the car and redirect

838
00:25:21,895 --> 00:25:24,141
the four hundred fifty dollar payment.

839
00:25:24,321 --> 00:25:26,751
They also send half of every raise

840
00:25:26,751 --> 00:25:27,537
to savings.

841
00:25:27,887 --> 00:25:29,882
Within two years they have a full

842
00:25:29,882 --> 00:25:30,789
emergency fund.

843
00:25:30,969 --> 00:25:33,502
Then they start investing six hundred dollars

844
00:25:33,502 --> 00:25:33,953
a month.

845
00:25:34,133 --> 00:25:36,182
At a seven percent illustration,

846
00:25:36,182 --> 00:25:38,615
that grows to roughly one hundred four

847
00:25:38,615 --> 00:25:40,536
thousand dollars in ten years,

848
00:25:40,536 --> 00:25:42,329
on top of their home equity.

849
00:25:42,509 --> 00:25:43,934
By their mid forties,

850
00:25:43,934 --> 00:25:45,969
they're solidly on level five.

851
00:25:46,149 --> 00:25:48,198
Nobody around them noticed anything,

852
00:25:48,198 --> 00:25:50,417
because they never changed the way they

853
00:25:50,417 --> 00:25:50,758
lived.

854
00:25:50,938 --> 00:25:52,308
They just stopped upgrading.

855
00:25:52,659 --> 00:25:53,785
Example three.

856
00:25:53,965 --> 00:25:55,613
Robert is fifty two.

857
00:25:55,793 --> 00:25:58,329
He has about one hundred fifty thousand

858
00:25:58,329 --> 00:26:00,735
dollars in a retirement account and a

859
00:26:00,735 --> 00:26:01,710
paid off house,

860
00:26:01,710 --> 00:26:03,140
so he's on level five,

861
00:26:03,140 --> 00:26:05,546
but he's worried he started too late.

862
00:26:05,726 --> 00:26:07,636
He's in his peak earning years,

863
00:26:07,636 --> 00:26:10,164
so he raises his contributions to fifteen

864
00:26:10,164 --> 00:26:12,382
hundred dollars a month for the next

865
00:26:12,382 --> 00:26:13,307
thirteen years.

866
00:26:13,657 --> 00:26:15,715
At a seven percent illustration,

867
00:26:15,715 --> 00:26:18,353
his one hundred fifty thousand plus those

868
00:26:18,353 --> 00:26:21,505
contributions could grow to roughly seven hundred

869
00:26:21,505 --> 00:26:23,885
fifty thousand dollars by sixty five.

870
00:26:24,065 --> 00:26:25,396
Add Social Security,

871
00:26:25,396 --> 00:26:26,793
and a paid off house,

872
00:26:26,793 --> 00:26:28,590
and his level seven number,

873
00:26:28,590 --> 00:26:29,920
the amount he needs,

874
00:26:29,920 --> 00:26:31,983
is much smaller than he feared.

875
00:26:32,163 --> 00:26:33,870
Late isn't the same as never.

876
00:26:34,050 --> 00:26:35,927
It just means your monthly number does

877
00:26:35,927 --> 00:26:36,766
more of the work.

878
00:26:37,117 --> 00:26:39,405
Look at what these three examples have

879
00:26:39,405 --> 00:26:40,007
in common.

880
00:26:40,187 --> 00:26:41,546
Nobody won the lottery.

881
00:26:41,726 --> 00:26:43,491
Nobody picked a magic stock.

882
00:26:43,671 --> 00:26:45,026
Each one found a gap,

883
00:26:45,026 --> 00:26:47,607
protected it with an automatic transfer,

884
00:26:47,607 --> 00:26:50,060
and refused to let lifestyle creep eat

885
00:26:50,060 --> 00:26:50,253
it.

886
00:26:50,433 --> 00:26:53,015
The level changed because the behavior changed,

887
00:26:53,015 --> 00:26:55,322
and the behavior changed because the money

888
00:26:55,322 --> 00:26:56,145
became visible.

889
00:26:56,496 --> 00:26:57,970
Climbing is one thing.

890
00:26:58,150 --> 00:27:00,426
Not falling back down is another.

891
00:27:00,606 --> 00:27:03,253
Here are five mistakes that knock people

892
00:27:03,253 --> 00:27:04,114
down a level,

893
00:27:04,114 --> 00:27:05,041
sometimes two.

894
00:27:05,391 --> 00:27:06,366
Mistake one.

895
00:27:06,546 --> 00:27:09,390
No emergency fund once you start investing.

896
00:27:09,570 --> 00:27:11,328
When the surprise comes,

897
00:27:11,328 --> 00:27:14,037
people sell investments at a bad time

898
00:27:14,037 --> 00:27:16,014
or go back to credit cards.

899
00:27:16,194 --> 00:27:17,215
Mistake two.

900
00:27:17,395 --> 00:27:19,253
A car payment that's too big.

901
00:27:19,433 --> 00:27:20,995
Cars lose value fast,

902
00:27:20,995 --> 00:27:23,151
and a large loan can drag you

903
00:27:23,151 --> 00:27:25,679
from level four back to level two.

904
00:27:26,030 --> 00:27:27,086
Mistake three.

905
00:27:27,266 --> 00:27:29,147
Panic selling in a market drop.

906
00:27:29,327 --> 00:27:31,973
Broad markets have fallen twenty percent or

907
00:27:31,973 --> 00:27:32,957
more many times,

908
00:27:32,957 --> 00:27:36,034
and recovered, though past results don't guarantee

909
00:27:36,034 --> 00:27:36,711
the future.

910
00:27:36,891 --> 00:27:39,319
Selling at the bottom turns a temporary

911
00:27:39,319 --> 00:27:41,001
drop into a permanent loss.

912
00:27:41,181 --> 00:27:42,284
Mistake four.

913
00:27:42,464 --> 00:27:44,995
Co-signing loans for friends or family.

914
00:27:45,175 --> 00:27:46,546
If they don't pay,

915
00:27:46,546 --> 00:27:47,079
you do.

916
00:27:47,429 --> 00:27:48,845
And mistake five.

917
00:27:49,025 --> 00:27:50,871
Falling for a scam or a too

918
00:27:50,871 --> 00:27:52,717
good to be true investment.

919
00:27:52,897 --> 00:27:55,613
The Federal Reserve found that about one

920
00:27:55,613 --> 00:27:58,125
in five adults experienced fraud or a

921
00:27:58,125 --> 00:27:59,619
scam in a single year.

922
00:27:59,799 --> 00:28:01,124
The higher your level,

923
00:28:01,124 --> 00:28:03,050
the more of a target you become.

924
00:28:03,230 --> 00:28:06,276
If someone promises guaranteed high returns,

925
00:28:06,276 --> 00:28:06,968
walk away.

926
00:28:07,319 --> 00:28:08,333
Before the recap,

927
00:28:08,333 --> 00:28:10,303
let me answer the questions I get

928
00:28:10,303 --> 00:28:11,974
the most about these levels.

929
00:28:12,325 --> 00:28:13,160
Question one.

930
00:28:13,340 --> 00:28:14,455
Does my home count?

931
00:28:14,635 --> 00:28:15,845
For net worth,

932
00:28:15,845 --> 00:28:16,191
yes.

933
00:28:16,371 --> 00:28:18,829
Your home's value minus your mortgage is

934
00:28:18,829 --> 00:28:19,505
part of it.

935
00:28:19,685 --> 00:28:21,063
But for these levels,

936
00:28:21,063 --> 00:28:23,161
I'd look mostly at money you can

937
00:28:23,161 --> 00:28:25,653
actually use: savings and investments.

938
00:28:25,833 --> 00:28:28,149
A big house with no savings is

939
00:28:28,149 --> 00:28:30,465
still level two in daily life.

940
00:28:30,816 --> 00:28:31,721
Question two.

941
00:28:31,901 --> 00:28:33,062
Can I skip a level?

942
00:28:33,242 --> 00:28:33,997
Not really.

943
00:28:34,177 --> 00:28:36,177
People try to jump from level two

944
00:28:36,177 --> 00:28:37,753
straight to investing big,

945
00:28:37,753 --> 00:28:38,844
without a cushion.

946
00:28:39,024 --> 00:28:41,243
Then the first emergency sends them back

947
00:28:41,243 --> 00:28:41,520
down.

948
00:28:41,700 --> 00:28:43,741
Each level builds the floor for the

949
00:28:43,741 --> 00:28:44,266
next one.

950
00:28:44,446 --> 00:28:45,468
Question three.

951
00:28:45,648 --> 00:28:47,169
Is it too late at fifty?

952
00:28:47,349 --> 00:28:47,592
No.

953
00:28:47,772 --> 00:28:49,855
Your monthly savings need to do more

954
00:28:49,855 --> 00:28:50,549
of the work,

955
00:28:50,549 --> 00:28:52,747
but people in their fifties often earn

956
00:28:52,747 --> 00:28:54,135
the most they ever will.

957
00:28:54,485 --> 00:28:55,379
Question four.

958
00:28:55,559 --> 00:28:57,126
What if I have a low income?

959
00:28:57,306 --> 00:28:59,448
Then the early levels are about protecting

960
00:28:59,448 --> 00:29:00,162
what you have.

961
00:29:00,342 --> 00:29:02,074
Avoid high interest debt,

962
00:29:02,074 --> 00:29:03,946
build even a small cushion,

963
00:29:03,946 --> 00:29:06,094
and look for ways to raise your

964
00:29:06,094 --> 00:29:08,519
income, like new skills or a better

965
00:29:08,519 --> 00:29:09,282
paying job.

966
00:29:09,462 --> 00:29:11,112
Small amounts still count,

967
00:29:11,112 --> 00:29:13,335
because the habit is what moves you

968
00:29:13,335 --> 00:29:13,525
up.

969
00:29:13,875 --> 00:29:14,827
Question five.

970
00:29:15,007 --> 00:29:17,010
Should I invest or pay off debt

971
00:29:17,010 --> 00:29:17,398
first?

972
00:29:17,578 --> 00:29:19,447
It depends on the interest rate.

973
00:29:19,627 --> 00:29:22,318
Debt above roughly eight to ten percent,

974
00:29:22,318 --> 00:29:23,865
like most credit cards,

975
00:29:23,865 --> 00:29:25,210
usually comes first,

976
00:29:25,210 --> 00:29:27,698
because paying it off is a guaranteed

977
00:29:27,698 --> 00:29:29,043
return at that rate.

978
00:29:29,223 --> 00:29:30,162
Low rate debt,

979
00:29:30,162 --> 00:29:31,502
like some mortgages,

980
00:29:31,502 --> 00:29:33,380
can sit alongside investing.

981
00:29:33,560 --> 00:29:35,133
And in almost every case,

982
00:29:35,133 --> 00:29:37,022
grab any employer match first,

983
00:29:37,022 --> 00:29:39,666
because that's an instant return you won't

984
00:29:39,666 --> 00:29:40,862
find anywhere else.

985
00:29:41,213 --> 00:29:42,269
Question six.

986
00:29:42,449 --> 00:29:44,225
How often should I check my level?

987
00:29:44,405 --> 00:29:46,319
Once a month is plenty for most

988
00:29:46,319 --> 00:29:46,751
people.

989
00:29:46,931 --> 00:29:48,010
Pick the same day,

990
00:29:48,010 --> 00:29:50,170
maybe the first Sunday of the month.

991
00:29:50,350 --> 00:29:52,355
Write down your four numbers:

992
00:29:52,355 --> 00:29:54,499
savings rate, months of runway,

993
00:29:54,499 --> 00:29:56,920
growth compared with contributions,

994
00:29:56,920 --> 00:29:58,372
and years of freedom.

995
00:29:58,552 --> 00:30:00,720
Checking every day makes you react to

996
00:30:00,720 --> 00:30:01,072
noise.

997
00:30:01,252 --> 00:30:03,430
Checking once a month shows you the

998
00:30:03,430 --> 00:30:05,733
trend, and the trend is what matters.

999
00:30:06,083 --> 00:30:07,000
Let's recap.

1000
00:30:07,180 --> 00:30:08,766
Level one, underwater,

1001
00:30:08,766 --> 00:30:10,640
you owe more than you own.

1002
00:30:10,820 --> 00:30:13,060
Level two, paycheck to paycheck.

1003
00:30:13,240 --> 00:30:15,353
Level three, the starter cushion.

1004
00:30:15,533 --> 00:30:17,045
Level four, stable,

1005
00:30:17,045 --> 00:30:20,067
with a full emergency fund and regular

1006
00:30:20,067 --> 00:30:20,862
investing.

1007
00:30:21,213 --> 00:30:22,754
Level five, momentum,

1008
00:30:22,754 --> 00:30:25,102
where your money does real work.

1009
00:30:25,282 --> 00:30:26,811
Level six, millionaire,

1010
00:30:26,811 --> 00:30:28,672
which isn't the finish line.

1011
00:30:28,852 --> 00:30:29,850
And level seven,

1012
00:30:29,850 --> 00:30:33,219
financially independent, when your investments can pay

1013
00:30:33,219 --> 00:30:35,714
for your life and work becomes optional.

1014
00:30:36,064 --> 00:30:37,863
And remember the three angles.

1015
00:30:38,043 --> 00:30:39,994
The numbers: your savings rate,

1016
00:30:39,994 --> 00:30:42,448
your runway, your growth and your years

1017
00:30:42,448 --> 00:30:43,140
of freedom.

1018
00:30:43,320 --> 00:30:45,215
The psychology: shame,

1019
00:30:45,215 --> 00:30:47,799
entitlement, comfort, boredom,

1020
00:30:47,799 --> 00:30:50,900
comparison, overconfidence and fear,

1021
00:30:50,900 --> 00:30:53,226
one feeling for each level,

1022
00:30:53,226 --> 00:30:55,034
pulling you sideways.

1023
00:30:55,214 --> 00:30:56,267
And the playbook:

1024
00:30:56,267 --> 00:30:58,063
a few simple moves this week,

1025
00:30:58,063 --> 00:30:59,301
for your level only.

1026
00:30:59,481 --> 00:31:01,582
If you remember nothing else from this

1027
00:31:01,582 --> 00:31:03,683
video, remember that your level is not

1028
00:31:03,683 --> 00:31:05,286
a verdict on you as a person.

1029
00:31:05,466 --> 00:31:07,378
It's just a starting point on a

1030
00:31:07,378 --> 00:31:09,351
map, and every level on that map

1031
00:31:09,351 --> 00:31:11,016
has a path to the next one.

1032
00:31:11,196 --> 00:31:13,752
Millions of people have walked it before

1033
00:31:13,752 --> 00:31:16,053
you, most of them with ordinary jobs

1034
00:31:16,053 --> 00:31:17,395
and ordinary incomes.

1035
00:31:17,746 --> 00:31:19,638
Now pick your level and do just

1036
00:31:19,638 --> 00:31:21,530
one thing: the move to the next

1037
00:31:21,530 --> 00:31:21,774
one.

1038
00:31:21,954 --> 00:31:22,500
That's it.

1039
00:31:22,680 --> 00:31:24,363
If you want the full plan,

1040
00:31:24,363 --> 00:31:25,205
step by step,

1041
00:31:25,205 --> 00:31:26,500
it's in my playbook.

1042
00:31:26,680 --> 00:31:28,352
The link is in the description.

1043
00:31:28,532 --> 00:31:30,556
And if you're on level three or

1044
00:31:30,556 --> 00:31:32,908
four, watch my video on what changes

1045
00:31:32,908 --> 00:31:34,998
at one hundred thousand dollars.

1046
00:31:35,178 --> 00:31:36,421
I'm Ryan Rover.

1047
00:31:36,601 --> 00:31:37,854
See you in the next one.
